<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1768">1768</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as First Russo-Turkish War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russo-Turkish War of 1768–1774 was a conflict between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Russian forces won major victories on land and at sea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> ended the war by granting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and making the Crimean Khanate independent of Ottoman control. Accounts of the Russian Empire treat it as a key stage in Russia's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nam-tien">southward expansion</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1768-1774.jpg" alt="Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)" width="320" height="188" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stefano Torelli · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Torelli2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of troubles in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. The Russian ambassador Nicholas Repnin and the Imperial Russian Army stood behind King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stanislaw-august-poniatowski">Stanisław August Poniatowski</a>, and Repnin imposed the Perpetual Treaty of 1768, which disadvantaged Poland and allowed Russian troops to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. The Bar Confederation, an alliance of noble, Roman Catholic and peasant rebels, was formed on 29 February 1768. When a band of confederates fled towards the Ottoman border, Russian pursuers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaporozhian-cossacks">Zaporozhian Cossacks</a>, clashed with Ottoman garrison troops. In the summer of 1768 Sultan Mustafa III heard that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> had razed the town of Balta, and on 6 October he imprisoned the Russian ambassador Aleksei Mikhailovich Obreskov and his staff, which marked the Ottoman declaration of war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Ottoman army was three times the size of the Russian one, but Grand Vizier Mehmed Emin Pasha proved militarily incompetent. In January 1769 a large Turkish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> army under the Crimean Khan Qırım Giray ravaged central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In September 1769 the Russians crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dniester">Dniester</a> into Moldavia, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khotyn">Khotyn</a>, entered Jassy on 7 October and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a> on 17 November. At Kagul on 1 August 1770 they routed the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>'s forces, and a third of the Ottoman troops are said to have drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>.</p><p>At sea, a Russian fleet under Count Alexei Grigoryevich Orlov entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> for the first time, and on 24 June 1770 twelve Russian ships destroyed twenty-two Ottoman vessels off Chesma with fire ships. Russian forces also crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, and the fleet intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, where it took part in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> and bombarded and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. A ceasefire began on 30 May 1772, but talks stalled over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. On 20 June 1774 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-suvorov">Alexander Suvorov</a> routed the Ottoman army near Kozludzha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> was signed on 21 July 1774. The Crimean Khanate formally became independent of both powers, though in practice it came to depend on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and was annexed in 1783. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> ceded the seaports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azov">Azov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kerch">Kerch</a>, giving Russia direct access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, along with the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-bug">Southern Bug</a>, and paid war <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> of 4.5 million rubles. Russia gained the status of protector of the Orthodox Christians of the Ottoman Empire. Northwestern Moldavia, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukovina">Bukovina</a>, passed to the Habsburg Empire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Russo-Turkish War (1768–1774)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>Russo-Turkish War</b> of 1768–1774 Russian forces occupied the peninsula in 1771.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the <b>Russo-Turkish War</b> of 1768–1774, a Russian force under Gottlieb Totleben campaigned in western Georgia, and Solomon took the opportunity to drive Ottoman garrisons from fortresses in his kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Catherine II's armies, under Field Marshals Rumyantsev and Suvorov, proved highly effective against the Ottomans in the <b>Russo-Turkish Wars</b> of 1768–1774 and 1787–1792.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
